# 23. Functional Acceptance Tests

Before declaring the application complete, implement and test the following scenarios.

### Test 1: Authentication

- An unauthenticated visitor cannot access the dashboard.
- A valid user can log in and log out.
- A deactivated user cannot log in.
- Protected APIs reject unauthorized requests.

### Test 2: Role-Based Access

- Staff can view SOPs and submit available forms.
- Staff cannot approve requests.
- Managers with approval authority can approve assigned requests.
- IT Staff can manage IT assets.
- General users cannot modify IT asset records.
- IT Staff cannot close tickets without the required permission.
- The Head of IT can reopen and close tickets.
- System administrators cannot approve business requests unless separately authorized.

### Test 3: Form Numbering

- The first submitted form in a numbering scope receives sequence 000.
- Subsequent submissions increment the sequence.
- Different categories have independent numbering sequences.
- A new month starts a new monthly sequence.
- Concurrent submissions cannot generate duplicate numbers.
- Cancelled and rejected forms retain their assigned numbers.
- Drafts do not receive official numbers unless configured to do so.

### Test 4: Approval Workflow

- A request is assigned to the correct approver.
- Unauthorized users cannot approve it.
- Approval actions record the approver and timestamp.
- Rejection requires a reason.
- Returned forms can be corrected and resubmitted.
- Approval history remains available.

### Test 5: Automatic Ticket Creation

- An approved form generates a ticket when configured.
- The ticket category matches the configured mapping.
- The ticket contains the original form reference.
- Retrying a failed process does not create duplicate tickets.
- Failed ticket creation does not erase the approval decision.

### Test 6: Ticket Management

- End users can create tickets and view their own tickets.
- IT Staff can update permitted ticket statuses.
- Internal notes are hidden from end users.
- Unauthorized status transitions are rejected.
- Only authorized users can reopen or close tickets.
- All status changes appear in the ticket history.

### Test 7: Asset Management

- IT Staff can create and update asset records.
- General users can view permitted asset information but cannot modify it.
- Asset transfers retain the previous assignment history.
- Asset searches and reports return correct records.

### Test 8: Document Management

- Authorized users can upload and publish SOPs.
- Forms can link to SOPs.
- Revised SOPs retain historical versions.
- Unauthorized users cannot access restricted documents.

### Test 9: Audit Trail

- Important operations generate audit records.
- Audit records preserve actor, timestamp, and relevant changes.
- Ordinary users cannot modify or delete audit records.

### Test 10: Responsive Design

- All primary modules work on desktop, tablet, and mobile browsers.
- Forms can be completed on mobile devices.
- Tables remain usable on small screens.
- Navigation works on all supported screen sizes.

### Test 11: Persistence

- Business records survive browser refreshes.
- Data remains available after users log out and back in.
- Records persist after application restarts.
- Database backup and restoration procedures are documented and tested.

